--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party software distributed with the Open DJ desktop app
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The Open DJ desktop app (the dmg) bundles third-party Python, JavaScript
and Rust components, a relocatable CPython runtime, a model checkpoint and
a font. Each remains under its own license. The complete inventory, with
each component's license identifier and the license and NOTICE texts its
distribution carries, ships inside the app at
Contents/Resources/payload/THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt (next to this NOTICE
and LICENSE). It is generated from the shipped payload by
scripts/third_party_licenses.py during the dmg build (`just dmg`), so it
cannot drift from what the app contains. `just third-party-licenses-flags`
prints the components whose licenses are copyleft or non-commercial.

Notices that Apache-2.0 section 4(d) requires us to reproduce, from
bundled components that ship a NOTICE file (the full set, regenerated on
every build, is the NOTICES section of THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt):

* tinytag
    Homepage: https://github.com/tinytag/tinytag
    License:  MIT (Copyright (c) 2014-2026 Tom Wallroth, Mat (mathiascode),
              et al.)
    Role:     audio tag reads (title / artist / album / genre / comment /
              BPM / key / ISRC / artwork / duration) for MP3, FLAC, MP4,
              AIFF, WAV, OGG and Opus. Its LICENSE ships in its wheel's
              dist-info, which the packaged app keeps.

Writing tags into audio files is not a product feature. mutagen is not a
dependency; see the note on mutagen below.

* requests (Apache-2.0)
    Requests
    Copyright 2019 Kenneth Reitz

* retrying (Apache-2.0)
    Copyright 2013 Ray Holder
    Copyright 2015 the "retrying" Contributors

Bundled outside the language package managers:

* CPython, via python-build-standalone (PSF-2.0)
    https://github.com/astral-sh/python-build-standalone
* Anybody variable font, wordmark subset (SIL OFL 1.1)
    Its license text is bundled with the font files.
* CPJKU/beat_this model weights: see "Beat detection model shipped in the
  desktop app" below.
* rekordbox_pdb.py, vendored crate-digger Kaitai-generated parser (EPL-1.0)
    apps/sync/usb/pioneer/_vendor/rekordbox_pdb.py
    https://github.com/Deep-Symmetry/crate-digger
    Attribution: Henry Betts, Fabian Lesniak, James Elliott / Deep Symmetry.
    The EPL-1.0 text ships in THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt.

Tag reading in the source dependency set uses tinytag (MIT). Mutagen
(GPL-2.0-or-later; https://github.com/quodlibet/mutagen) is not declared
in the current pyproject.toml or uv.lock, and there is no tags extra.
For the components actually distributed in a desktop payload, consult
that payload's generated THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt inventory.

The Python dependency set for source installs is declared in
pyproject.toml and locked in uv.lock; reqs.json and requirements.txt carry
the development superset.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Open-source prior art, format specs, and reverse-engineering references
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

music-dj-tools is built against a body of open-source work that documented
DJ-library file formats, reverse-engineered vendor protocols, and shipped
interop code long before this project existed. None of their code is
redistributed here except crate-digger (a vendored generated parser) and
Beat This! (package and weights), but our readers, writers, and test
oracles cross-check against their specs and implementations. Gratitude
to the authors.

See docs/prior-art-and-communities.md for the full annotated list.

Rekordbox master.db (v6 / v7)
* dylanljones/pyrekordbox (MIT)
    https://github.com/dylanljones/pyrekordbox
    Reference implementation and documentation for the SQLCipher-encrypted
    master.db, XML export, ANLZ analysis files, and MySettings.

Rekordbox USB export (export.pdb / ANLZ)
* Deep-Symmetry/crate-digger (Eclipse Public License 1.0)
    https://github.com/Deep-Symmetry/crate-digger
    Kaitai Struct .ksy specifications for export.pdb and ANLZ; the
    canonical machine-readable format definitions we validate against.
    A Kaitai-generated parser derived from its rekordbox_pdb.ksy IS
    redistributed (apps/sync/usb/pioneer/_vendor/rekordbox_pdb.py,
    EPL-1.0, attribution Henry Betts, Fabian Lesniak, James Elliott /
    Deep Symmetry; source at https://github.com/Deep-Symmetry/crate-digger).
    The EPL-1.0 text is in THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt.
* Holzhaus/rekordcrate (MIT)
    https://github.com/Holzhaus/rekordcrate
    Rust library for Rekordbox USB export; reference for the USB write
    path.
* Deep-Symmetry/dysentery (Eclipse Public License 1.0)
    https://github.com/Deep-Symmetry/dysentery
    Pioneer ProDJ Link protocol reverse engineering; canonical analysis
    docs at djl-analysis.deepsymmetry.org.
* flesniak/python-prodj-link (GPL-3.0)
    https://github.com/flesniak/python-prodj-link
    Python ProDJ Link implementation; credited by crate-digger as an
    early PDB format contributor.

Serato crates and tags
* Holzhaus/serato-tags (MIT)
    https://github.com/Holzhaus/serato-tags
    Canonical spec for Serato GEOB ID3 tags: Markers2, BeatGrid, Overview.
* Holzhaus/triseratops (Mozilla Public License 2.0)
    https://github.com/Holzhaus/triseratops
    Rust Serato tag parser and writer; complements our Serato write path.

Traktor NML
* wolkenarchitekt/traktor-nml-utils (MIT)
    https://github.com/wolkenarchitekt/traktor-nml-utils
    Traktor NML XML parser (Python); reference schema for our Traktor
    import and export path.

Engine DJ (Denon)
* xsco/libdjinterop (LGPL-3.0)
    https://github.com/xsco/libdjinterop
    C++ library for Engine Library read and write (Denon SC Prime series);
    the only open-source library covering the Engine DJ format end to
    end.

CDJ network protocol (live link)
* Deep-Symmetry/beat-link (Eclipse Public License 1.0)
    https://github.com/Deep-Symmetry/beat-link
    Java library for Pioneer ProDJ Link, waveform streaming, and live
    CDJ sync.

djay Pro MediaLibrary.db
* yapstudios/YapDatabase (MIT)
    https://github.com/yapstudios/YapDatabase
    Objective-C key-value store over SQLite used internally by djay Pro;
    understanding its layout unlocks djay library access.

Peer open-source DJ software
* mixxxdj/mixxx (GPL-2.0-or-later)
    https://github.com/mixxxdj/mixxx
    Gold-standard open-source DJ application; its library import code is
    a bugs-as-oracle reference for our Serato and Traktor readers.

Audio analysis (reference oracles for beat, key, onset)
* CPJKU/madmom (code: BSD-3-Clause; pre-trained models: CC-BY-NC-SA 4.0)
    https://github.com/CPJKU/madmom
    Beat tracking, onset detection, key estimation; used at runtime and
    as an analysis-quality oracle. The Python package is BSD-3-Clause,
    but the shipped pre-trained model files (``madmom/models/*``) are
    released under Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike
    4.0 International (CC-BY-NC-SA 4.0). Non-commercial use in this
    project is fine; redistribution of any product that bundles those
    model files requires explicit compliance with CC-BY-NC-SA 4.0.
    madmom is installed from git via requirements.txt (analysis extra)
    and is never bundled in the music-dj-tools wheel.

Beat detection model shipped in the desktop app
* CPJKU/beat_this (code and published model weights: MIT)
    https://github.com/CPJKU/beat_this
    Copyright (c) 2024 Institute of Computational Perception, JKU Linz,
    Austria. Unlike the projects above, this one IS redistributed: the
    desktop engine payload bundles the ``final0`` checkpoint at
    ``models/beatgrid/beat_this-final0.ckpt`` with its MIT notice beside
    it (``models/beatgrid/LICENSE-beat_this.txt``), and the runner site
    installs the ``beat-this`` package. Upstream states the weights are
    MIT; its training audio is under mixed terms that upstream leaves to
    the user to assess. See
    docs/decisions/ADR-NEW-beat-this-weights-license.md.

No code from the projects above is copied into this repository. (Beat This!, above, is an exception: its package and weights are bundled. crate-digger, above, is an exception: a Kaitai-generated parser is vendored.) They are
cited here because their documentation, file-format specs, and issue
trackers directly informed our implementation and are the references we
return to when something does not match.
